  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NESA Center Cyber Security and Law Enforcement Seminar 01-23</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Cybercrimes are increasing worldwide and news about extensive data breaches are often in the headlines. Cyber threats are constantly growing and evolving. The complexity and scope of these crimes are beyond the capacity of traditional police institutions. Organized criminal syndicates as well as state actors are using this domain to exploit sensitive information and disseminate misinformation for political or financial gains. Fighting this new menace requires digital evidence and advanced technological tools requiring increased financial resources. Needs of police organizations have multiplied requiring more IT specialists as well as enhanced technical training for its investigators. This requires both institutional and cultural changes to take effect as police institutions need revamping. Which institutions should join hands and coordinate? What kind of institutional transformation is required to deal with this crisis and what is the role of inter-agency cooperation for instance, with national security organizations and law enforcement institutions in confronting cyber threats from international locations. The seminar will deliberate on the nature and scope of these challenges and search for best practices in the arena.</narrative>
  </description>
